# Pristine Metal-Organic Frameworks and their Composites for Energy Storage and Conversion

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a new class of crystalline porous organic-inorganic hybrid materials, have recently attracted increasing interest in the field of energy storage and conversion. Herein, recent progress of MOFs and MOF composites for energy storage and conversion applications, including photochemical and electrochemical fuel production (hydrogen production and CO2 reduction), water oxidation, supercapacitors, and Li-based batteries (Li-ion, Li-S, and Li-O2 batteries), is summarized. Typical development strategies (e.g., incorporation of active components, design of smart morphologies, and judicious selection of organic linkers and metal nodes) of MOFs and MOF composites for particular energy storage and conversion applications are highlighted. A broad overview of recent progress is provided, which will hopefully promote the future development of MOFs and MOF composites for advanced energy storage and conversion applications.

Dedicated to Professor Susumu Kitagawa on the occasion of his 65th birthday
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a new class of crystalline porous organic– inorganic hybrid materials, have recently attracted increasing interest in the field of energy storage and conversion. Herein, recent progress of MOFs and MOF composites for energy storage and conversion applications, including photochemical and electrochemical fuel production (hydrogen production and $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ reduction), water oxidation, supercapacitors, and Li-based batteries (Li-ion, Li–S, and Li– $\cdot\mathsf{o}_{2}$ batteries), is summarized. Typical development strategies (e.g., incorporation of active components, design of smart morphologies, and judicious selection of organic linkers and metal nodes) of MOFs and MOF composites for particular energy storage and conversion applications are highlighted. A broad overview of recent progress is provided, which will hopefully promote the future development of MOFs and MOF composites for advanced energy storage and conversion applications.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), also denoted as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), represent a new class of crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid porous materials.[12–16] MOFs have been widely studied as functional materials for various applications including selective gas adsorption and separation,[17] hydrogen storage,[18–20] chemical sensors,[21] heterogeneous catalysis,[22] and drug delivery,[23] due to their unique and advantageous characteristics including high surface area, regular and tunable pore structures, diversity in the chemical compositions of the organic linkers and metal nodes, facile synthesis, as well as the controllable chemi­ stry and functionality.[20,24–26] Recently, MOF-based materials, including pristine MOFs, MOF composites (e.g., Pt nanoparticles ${\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ and MOF/graphene composite), and MOF-derived materials, have attracted numerous interests in the field of energy storage and conversion.[27–36] There have been many excellent reviews published by us and others mainly focusing on MOF-derived materials such as carbon, metal/carbon, and metal oxide materials for various energy storage and conversion applications.[28,31,37–43] Zou and co-workers have summarized recent progress of MOF-based nanomaterials for typical electrocatalysis applications including electrocatalysis of oxygen reduction reaction, oxygen evolution reaction, hydrogen evolution reaction, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction.[37] Yamauchi and co-workers summarized recent developments of the MOFderived porous metal oxide nanomaterials for supercapacitors, and utilization of MOFs as templates to fabricate nanoarchitectured porous materials for energy storage and conversion applications[32,43] Therefore, MOF-derived materials will not be discussed in detail in this review. Instead, more exhaustive and insightful reviews focusing on pristine MOFs and MOF composites for energy storage and conversion applications will be presented. In the following sections, recent progress of MOFs and MOF composites for photochemical and electrochemical fuel production (hydrogen production and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction) and water oxidation, supercapacitors, and Li-based batteries (Li-ion batteries, Li–S batteries, and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries) is summarized, and typical development strategies of pristine MOFs and MOF composites for high-performance energy storage and conversion applications will be discussed.
## 2. Photochemical Fuel Production and Water Oxidation
Artificial photosynthesis is considered a promising sustainable solution to meet the increasing energy demand and address the severe climate change issues.[1,44–46] In artificial photo­ synthesis, solar energy is harvested and water or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is reduced to chemical fuels (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , HCOOH, and CO) along with the oxidation of water into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Apparently, artificial photo­ synthesis can not only decrease the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , one of the most abundant greenhouse gases, in the atmosphere, but also produce useful chemical fuels with high energy density that are convenient to transport and store and can be subsequently used to generate electricity through electrochemical devices like fuel cells. An artificial photosynthesis system is supposed to include photo­sensitizers for light harvesting and catalysts for both water/ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water oxidation in a single cell. However, to date it is still a challenge to fabricate such a system due to the difficulty of integrating multi­functional units into a single device.[1,47] Therefore, efforts were devoted to developing photo­ catalytic systems for separated half reactions of water reduction (hydrogen production), $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water oxidation (Figure 1A,B), and great progress has been made in design and utilization of enzymes, semiconductors, and transition metal complexes for catalysis of these 3 half reactions.[45,48–51] As a new class of crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid porous materials, MOFs and their composites also attracted enormous interests for photochemical fuel production and water oxidation because of the unique advantages:[52–54] (1) the light adsorption properties of MOFs can be well-tuned through introduction of functional groups such as amino and porphyrin groups; (2) MOFs can serve as substrates or hosts for incorporation of molecular photocatalytically active complexes to afford heterogeneous photocatalysts with desirable recyclability and stability; (3) the well-defined pore structure of MOFs can guarantee the monodispersion of the active components, thus preventing the decomposition and deactivation of the active components through multimolecular pathway; (4) the high surface area and porosity ensure the intimate contact between MOFs and the incorporated active components such as nanoparticles and complexes, leading to efficient charge separation and electron transfer; (5) incorporation of more than one active components into a single MOF crystal is possible to afford multifunctional MOF-based composites where synergistic effects between the incorporated components can enhance the photo­catalytic performance. In the following sections, MOFs and MOF composites for photochemical hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and water oxidation will be discussed respectively, mainly focusing
