## 2.1. Photochemical Hydrogen Production
A photocatalytic system for hydrogen production involves at least two basic components: photosensitizers for light harvest to generate electron–hole pairs and catalysts for hydrogen production driven by the photogenerated electrons. Development strategies of MOFs and MOF composites for photocatalysis of hydrogen production mainly focus on incorporation of photosensitive and catalytic components into MOFs, aiming to afford MOF-based photocatalysts with high visible light harvest efficiency, desirable catalytic activity for hydrogen generation, and excellent stability during long-term operation. Typical development strategies include incorporation of functional groups onto organic linkers, precious metal nanoparticles, Earth-abundant nanoparticles, coordination complexes, and polyoxometalates into MOFs, which will be discussed in detail in the following sections. The photocatalytic hydrogen production performances of recent MOFs and MOF composites are summarized in Table 1.
## 2.1.1. Incorporation of Functional Groups onto the Organic Linkers
Porphyrin is another functional moiety with visible light photo­response that can be incorporated onto the organic linkers of MOFs. In 2012, Rosseinsky and co-workers presented the first example using porphyrin incorporated MOF for photocatalysis of hydrogen production.[58] They used free-base mesotetra(4-carboxyl-phenyl) porphyrin $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP})$ as linkers and Al cations as metal centers to construct this porphyrin incorporated MOF (denoted as Al-PMOF, Figure 2C) with light absorption band in visible light region. The photocatalytic activity of Al-PMOF was investigated in the Al-PMOF/ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA)/Pt system where EDTA served as the sacrificial electron donor and Pt provided hydrogen production catalytic sites. Under visible light irradiation, the excited porphyrin moieties of Al-PMOF reacted with the sacrificial electron donor EDTA and then transferred electrons to Pt, where hydrogen evolution reaction took place (Figure 2D). The introduction of porphyrin moieties also brings the advantage that the photo­ catalytic properties can be finely controlled through incorporation of different central metal ions in the porphyrin rings.
In another case, azobenzene moieties were incorporated onto the ligands to afford a dye-like ligand-based MOF with visible light response which can efficiently produce hydrogen with Ag as cocatalyst.[59]
## 2.1.2. Incorporation of Precious Metal Nanoparticles
As mentioned above, precious metal nanoparticles such as Pt, Ag, and Au were usually incorporated into the MOF photocatalysis systems. The incorporation of precious metal nanoparticles may enhance the photocatalytic performance in three aspects: (1) excited electrons can be produced under visible light irradiation through the excitation of the localized surface plasmon resonance of the metal nanoparticles, contributing to the visible light response; (2) the metal nanoparticles can trap electrons from MOFs, promoting the charge separation process and mitigating the electron–hole recombination; and (3) precious metals can provide highly active reaction sites for catalysis of the hydrogen production. The incorporation of precious metal nanoparticles in MOFs is often achieved by photoreduction or chemical reduction of the metal salts. A uniform size and spatial distribution of the metal particles throughout the MOF crystals may be favored. Jiang and co-workers investigated the influence of the spatial distribution of the Pt nanoparticles on the photocatalytic performance of hydrogen production.[60] Pt nanoparticles of ${\approx}3~\mathrm{nm}$ were incorporated inside or on the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ to get $\mathrm{Pt}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}$ UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ , respectively (Figure 3A–C). Based on the ultrafast transient absorption and time-resolved photo­ luminescence measurements, it was demonstrated that the charge separation was more efficient for $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{Pt/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ because of the shorter electron transfer distance from UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to the $\mathrm{Pt}$ nano­particles. Also, Pt nanoparticles inside the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ crystals in the case of $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are under stronger protection and confinement, preventing the Pt leaching and aggregation upon hydrogen production operation. As a consequence, $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibited much higher photocatalytic activity as well as better stability and recyclability for hydrogen production than $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure 3D,E).
## 2.1.3. Incorporation of Earth-Abundant Nanoparticles
Although precious metal nanoparticles can remarkably enhance the photocatalytic performance, their widespread utilization is greatly hindered by their scarcity and high cost. Therefore, replacing precious metal with Earth-abundant nanoparticles without compromise of the photocatalytic activity is highly desirable. For example, Lu and co-workers used an in situ chemical reduction method to incorporate Ni nanoparticles of ${\approx}9~\mathrm{nm}$ into MOF-5 with a high dispersion of ${\approx}42\%$ .[61] The $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ composite showed high photocatalytic activity for hydrogen production comparable to $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ using triethanolamine (TEOA) as sacrificial electron donor and Eosin Y as photosensitizer under visible light irradiation. Nonprecious metal alloys can also be introduced into MOF for photocatalytic hydrogen production. Lu and co-workers used the double solvents method and subsequent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reduction to incorporate MoNi alloy nanoparticles into MIL-101 $(\mathrm{MoNi@MIL}{\cdot}101$ ).[62] The resulting $\mathrm{MoNi@MIL-101}$ composite showed a better photocatalytic activity toward hydrogen production $(\approx740\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}},$ ) under visible light irradiation $(520\ \mathrm{nm})$ than those of monometal $\mathrm{Ni}@$ MIL-101 or $\mathbf{Mo}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ , which can be ascribed to the lower hydrogen adsorption free energy of $\mathrm{MoNi@MIL-101}$ based on density functional theory (DFT) and frontier molecular orbital (FMO) theory calculations.
Non-precious metal chalcogenides such as NiO,[63] $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ ,[64] and $\mathrm{CdS^{[64-66]}}$ were also incorporated into MOFs to improve the photocatalytic performance toward hydrogen production. Among these metal chalcogenides, CdS is the most frequently used one in the MOF photocatalysis systems because of its suitable band position and visible light photoresponse.[67,68] The CdS/MOF heterojunction can promote the separation of electrons and holes and mitigate the charge recombination. For example, the incorporation of CdS nanoparticles in MIL-101[66] and UiO- $66^{[65]}$ can lead to much enhanced photocatalytic performance for hydrogen production than the pristine CdS or the corresponding MOFs under visible light irradiation, which can be ascribed to both the efficient visible light absorption of CdS and the promoted photogenerated electron–hole separation on the CdS/MOF heterojunctions.
## 2.1.4. Incorporation of Precious Metal Complexes
Inspired by the molecular precious metal complexes that have been widely studied as photosensitizers and catalytic centers for hydrogen production in homogeneous photocatalytic systems,[69–71] many works have been done on incorporating precious metal complexes into MOFs aiming to introduce photo­sensitive sites for visible light harvesting and catalytic reaction sites for catalysis of hydrogen production.
In 2012, Lin and co-workers incorporated $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ $\langle{\mathrm{ppy}}=2$ -phenylpyridine) complexes with visible light response into a UiO MOF by using $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{ppy})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]^{+}$ -derived dicarboxylates as organic linkers (Figure 4A).[72] After loading of Pt nanoparticles as cocatalysts, this UiO MOF exhibited photocatalytic activity toward hydrogen production with turnover number (TON) of $1620\mathrm{in}6\mathrm{h}$ using triethylamine (TEA) as sacrificial electron donor under visible light irradiation. The photocatalytic processes of this system involved visible light harvest by the Ir-complexes incorporated onto the linkers, reduction of the excited Ir-complexes by TEA to form radicals, electron transfer from the unstable radicals to the Pt nanoparticles, and hydrogen production reactions on the active sites of the Pt nanoparticles (Figure 4B).
Apart from serving as photosensitizers, the precious metal complexes incorporated into MOFs can also act as catalysts for hydrogen production. For example, Xu and co-workers used a postsynthesis modification method to incorporate Pt-complexes onto the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine-based linkers of the Al-based MOF-253 (denoted as MOF-253-Pt, Figure 4C).[73] After the introduction of the $\mathrm{{Pt}}$ -complex, the absorption band of MOF-253-Pt shifted to the visible region, indicating the visible light response of the $\mathrm{\Pt}$ -complexes. Remarkably, the MOF-253-Pt showed photo­ catalytic activity for hydrogen production under visible light irradiation (wavelength $>420\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) even without the presence of cocatalysts such as Pt nanoparticles, because the Pt-complexes can also serve as catalysts for hydrogen production through the heterolytic coupling pathway (Figure 4D).
It is also possible to incorporate two kinds of precious metal complexes into one MOF crystal. It is desirable that one kind of the complexes can efficiently harvest visible light while the other one can act as highly active catalysts for hydrogen production, and they are spatially close enough to facilitate the electron transfer. The first example of MOFs incorporated with two kinds of precious metal complexes were presented by Chen and coworkers.[74] $[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)(bpy)}_{2}]^{2+}$ as photosensitizer and Pt(dcbpy) $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ as efficient hydrogen production catalyst were simultaneously incorporated into crystals of UiO-67, a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF, using a mix-and-match approach (Figure 4E). Under visible light irradiation, the Ru-complexes can be excited by the incident photons, and the facile electron transfer from the excited Ru-complexes to the $\mathrm{\Pt}$ -complexes catalysts, where hydrogen production reactions took place, was guaranteed due to the close spatial proximity of these two functional complexes. In another work, Park and co-workers incorporated Ir-complexes as photosensitizers and Pt-complexes as catalysts into bipyridine-embedded UiO67, which exhibited a very stable photocatalytic performance for hydrogen evolution over $6.5\mathrm{\d.}^{\left[75\right]}$ The extraordinary stability can be ascribed to the self-healing mechanism in this system, where the broken metal-diimine bonds can be reformed with the abundant diimine groups in the linkers of UiO-67.
## 2.1.5. Incorporation of Nonprecious Metal Complexes
The scarcity and high cost of the precious metals urge the researchers to develop nonprecious metal complexes that can replace the precious metal complexes without compromise of the photocatalytic activity. Indeed, natural hydrogenases with high catalytic activity toward hydrogen production are based on nonprecious metal complexes such as [FeFe] and [NiFe] hydrogenases.[76] In this regard, Ott and co-workers incorporated biomimetic [FeFe] complexes, [FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ (dcbdt $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1{,}4$ -dicarboxylbenzene-2,3-dithiolate), into UiO-66 through a postsynthetic exchange method (Figure 5A).[77] The resulting [FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ incorporated UiO-66 exhibited much better photocatalytic performance than UiO-66 and molecular [FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ with $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ as photosensitizer and ascorbate as electron donor under visible light irradiation (wavelength $=470\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). The enhanced photocatalytic performance of the [FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ incorporated UiO-66 than the homogeneous [FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ may be attributed to the reduced nonproductive charge recombination and the promoted disproportionation to form the dianionic $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ species with high catalytic activity. In 2014, Feng and coworkers incorporated another biomimetric [FeFe] complex $([(1^{\prime}\mathrm{-SCH_{2}})_{2}\mathrm{NC(O)C_{5}H_{4}N}]\mathrm{-[Fe_{2}(C O)_{6}])}$ into a zirconium-porphyrin MOF $(\mathrm{ZrPF})$ through the direct bonding of the [FeFe] complexes with the zinc centers of the porphyrin rings.[78]
The strong bonding between the photosensitizer porphyrins and the catalyst [FeFe] complexes ensured a facile electron transfer during photocatalytic hydrogen production, leading to enhanced performance compared with homogeneous [FeFe] complexes.
In another case, Gascon and co-workers incorporated the derivative complexes of cobaloxime as catalysts into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti) using a “Ship-in-a-bottle” strategy, which showed remarkable photocatalytic activity for hydrogen evolution under visible light irradiation as well as high recycling stability.[79] Recently, Huang and co-workers incorporated Ni-complexes as catalysts into an Al-based MOF through the coordination bonding of the Ni(II) cations with the nitrogen on the amino groups of the linkers and the oxygen in the ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedron metal nodes.[80] The resulting MOF showed much enhanced photocatalytic performance toward overall water splitting under
UV–vis light irradiation than the Al-based MOF without Nicomplexes incorporation.
## 2.1.6. Incorporation of Polyoxometalates
Polyoxometalates (POMs), inorganic polyatomic clusters composed of transition metal atoms connected with oxygen atoms, and their composites have been widely studied as active catalysts for hydrogen production.[81,82] The incorporation of POMs into the cavities of MOFs with visible light response may lead to extraordinary photocatalytic activity because of the synergistic effect between POMs and MOFs. In 2015, Lin and coworkers successfully incorporated the Wells–Dawson-type POM $[\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}$ into the cavities of the $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ -incorporated UiO MOF with visible light response (Figure 5B).[83] The $[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}$ moieties in the linkers can be excited by visible light, and the facile multielectron injection from the excited linkers to the POMs was guaranteed because each POM was encapsulated by six or twelve $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ -incorporated linkers in the tetrahedral or octahedral pores, respectively (Figure 5C). As a result, the resulting $\mathsf{P O M@M O F}$ composite delivered a desirable photocatalytic performance toward hydrogen production under visible light irradiation. In the next year, the same group further improved the performance of POM/ MOF composites by using the Ni-containing POM $\mathrm{([Ni_{4}(H_{2}O)}$ $_2(\mathrm{PW}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{34})_{2}]^{10-})$ with higher photocatalytic activity.[84] The TON toward hydrogen evolution of the resulting Ni-containing POM incorporated MOF under visible light irradiation with methanol as electron donor was as high as 1476.
## 2.2. Photochemical $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
Visible light driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is a promising method to convert solar energy into useful chemical fuels while reducing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ amount in the atmosphere.[85–87] Similar to hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction photocatalysis systems also require photosensitive components for light harvest and catalytic components for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reactions. The development strategies of MOFs for photocatalysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, which involve incorporation of functional groups, precious metal nanoparticles, earth-abundant materials, and precious/nonprecious metal coordination complexes into MOFs, are also similar to those of the hydrogen production, mainly aiming to incorporate efficient photosensitizers and highly active catalysts into MOFs. The photocatalytic performances of recent MOFs and MOF composites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction are summarized in Table 2.
## 2.2.1. Incorporation of Functional Groups onto the Organic Linkers
As mentioned above, the photoresponse of MOFs can be tuned to the visible light region by introduction of functional groups onto the linkers. In the case of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, for example, introduction of amino groups onto the BDC linkers of MIL-125(Ti) can afford ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125({\mathrm{Ti}})$ with visible light response.[88] In addition, the introduction of amino groups can also enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capability. As a result, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ can reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ under visible light irradiation using TEOA as sacrificial electron donor. A photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction mechanism, where the photoexcited linker transferred an electron to $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ to form ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was reduced by $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{H C O O^{-}}$ , was proposed.
In 2015, Jiang and co-workers incorporated visible light photo­sensitive porphyrin groups into a highly stable $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF, PCN-222, by using ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ as organic ligands.[89] PCN-222 showed a visible light response as well as a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, and thus a remarkable photocatalytic activity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction with production of $30\ \mathrm{\textmumol}$ ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ under visible light irradiation. The introduction of porphyrin groups into MOFs also enables atomically dispersed catalytic sites for photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ because single unsaturated metal cation such as ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ can be incorporated into the porphyrin ring.[90]
## 2.2.2. Incorporation of Precious Metal Nanoparticles
Incorporation of precious metal nanoparticles such as Ag, Pt, and Au into MOFs is also an efficient strategy to enhance the photocatalytic performance toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[91,92] Recently, Yaghi and co-workers synthesized $\mathsf{A g}$ nanocubes coated with Re-complexes incorporated MOF $({\mathrm{Ag}}{\mathrm{{CRe}}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}})$ ) for plasmonenhanced photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Figure 6).[91] Upon light irradiation, prominently intensified near-surface electric fields were generated on the surface of the Ag nanocubes and can subsequently be absorbed by the Re-complexes catalysts immobilized on the linkers of the ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ . It was found that the $\mathsf{A g c R e}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{M O F}$ with thinner ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ layer which underwent stronger near-surface electric fields provided better photo­ catalytic performance: a 5-fold improvement of photocatalytic activity for ${\tt A g}{\tt C R e}_{3}{\cdot}{\tt M O F}$ with ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ layer of $33\ \mathrm{nm}$ and 7-fold improvement for $\mathsf{A g c R e}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{M O F}$ with ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ layer of $16~\mathrm{nm}$ toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO under visible light irradiation over the ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ without Ag incorporation (Figure 6C). In another case, $\mathrm{Pt}$ and Au nanoparticles were doped into photosensitive ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{.}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125({\mathrm{Ti}})$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ under visible light irradiation.[92]
## 2.2.3. Incorporation of Earth-Abundant Materials
Incorporation of Earth-abundant materials is a better choice considering the high cost and scarcity of the precious metals. Many Earth-abundant materials have been introduced into MOFs for photocatalysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, including CdS,[93] $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[94,95] $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ ,[96] and graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ ,[97,98] which are mainly semiconductors that can be excited by visible light to generate electron–hole pairs for the following $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. CdS, a semiconductor that have been widely utilized for visible light photocatalysis, was coupled with Co-ZIF-9 for photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO by Wang and co-workers.[93] Under optimized conditions, a desirable apparent quantum yield of ${\approx}2\%$ was delivered. Also, the $^{13}{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ isotopic measurement confirmed that the generated CO was from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ rather than the organic compounds in this system. Another well-studied semiconductor $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , was combined with a Cu-based MOF $(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2})$ by constructing a $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}@$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core–shell structure (Figure 7A,B).[94] The macroporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell can be photoexcited to generate electron–hole pairs and can efficiently transfer electrons to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ cores with high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption capability, where $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was selectively reduced to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Figure 7C). The selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ can be ascribed to the Cu sites in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ with high catalytic selectivity and the efficient electron transfer from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ core for this eight-electron involving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction process. Graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ , an emerging polymeric conjugated semiconductor, can also serve as photosensitizer for visible light harvest.[99] Ye and co-workers used an electrostatic self-assembly method to coat negatively charged $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets on the surface of the positively charged UiO-66 MOF (denoted as UiO-66/CNNS).[97] The resulting UiO-66/CNNS composite exhibited much enhanced photocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO under visible light irradiation than bare $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets and UiO-66, which was due to the efficient charge transfer from the visible light photosensitive $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets to the UiO-66 MOF with high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capability (Figure 7D,E). $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ was also combined with Co-ZIF-9 for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO, where $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ acted as visible light harvester while Co-ZIF-9 served as cocatalyst (Figure 7F).[98]
## 2.2.4. Incorporation of Coordination Complexes
Incorporation of coordination complexes with visible light photoresponse and catalytic activity into MOF is an efficient strategy to afford MOF-based heterogeneous photocatalysts for visible light driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. In 2014, Luo and co-workers synthesized a Y-based MOF incorporated with Ircomplex units (Ir-CP) by using $\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{Hdcbpy)}$ as organic linkers.[100] The resulting Ir-CP showed a broad light absorption band in the UV–vis region as well as a long-lived excited state. It exhibited desirable photocatalytic activity (production of $38~\mathrm{\textmumol}$ $\operatorname{HCOO}^{-}$ in $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ ) and stability (five six-hour continuous runs without obvious activity loss) toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to $\mathrm{\textHCOO^{-}}$ under visible light irradiation. In addition to Ir-complexes, Re-complexes were also incorporated into MOF for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Lin and co-workers used Re-complexes $\mathrm{(Re(bpy)(CO)_{3}C l_{3}}$ , well known as photocatalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO) derived dicarboxylate ligands to construct the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based UiO MOF.[101] The resulting Re-incorporated UiO MOF showed improved photocatalytic stability for visible light driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO than the homogeneous Re-complexes that underwent severe decomposition during operation.
The aforementioned $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction systems were investigated under high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure, which is undesirable in terms of energetic and economic considerations. In this regard, Kitagawa and co-workers used a postsynthetic exchange method to prepare a Ru-complex incorporated $\mathrm{zr}$ -based MOF which can photocatalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to produce CO and HCOOH with high TON even at low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration $(5\%\ \mathrm{CO}_{2})$ .[102] The high photocatalytic activity of this Ru-complex incorporated MOF under low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration can be attributed to the synergistic effect between the MOF with high and selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption from gas mixture and the Ru-complexes with catalytic activity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
Besides precious metal complexes, Earth-abundant metal complexes with photocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction can also be introduced into MOFs. In 2015, Cohen and co-workers incorporated Mn-complexes $\left(\mathrm{Mn(dcbpy)(CO)_{3}B r}\right)$ into a $Z\mathrm{r}.$ based MOF through a postsynthetic metalation method.[103] The resulting Mn-complex incorporated MOF showed higher TON (50 and 110 over 4 and $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively) and selectivity $(96\%$ over 4 h) for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to HCOOH than the homogeneous Mn-complexes under visible light irradiation.
## 2.3. Photochemical Water Oxidation
Compared with the photocatalysis of hydrogen production and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, reported works on MOFs for photocatalysis of water oxidation are relatively limited, probably because most of the MOFs cannot survive the harsh conditions of water oxidation (e.g., strongly acidic electrolyte and strong oxidant).[7]
Apart from precious metal based complexes, biomimetric Earth-abundant metal based complexes with considerable photo­cataytic activity toward water oxidation can also be incorporated into MOFs to afford efficient and stable heterogeneous photocatalysts. In 2013, Das and co-workers successfully incorporated MnTD, a mimic of the oxygen-evolving complex of photosystem II with high-valent $\operatorname{Mn}({\upmu}{-}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ core, into the pores of MIL-101(Cr) (Figure 8B).[106] MnTD is known to suffer from severe degradation caused by the intermolecular interactions between the MnTD molecules.[107] MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ with unique cage-like pores can confine one MnTD molecule within one pore, thus prevented the degradation of MnTD induced by interactions between two or more MnTD molecules. The photocatalytic activity toward water oxidation was investigated using K-oxone as electron acceptor and the MnTD incorporated MIL-101(Cr) as photocatalyst. It was found that oxygen was produced continuously at a constant rate over 6 h operation (with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ deoxygenation and addition of K-oxone when the electrode was saturated with ${\bf O}_{2}^{\prime}$ , indicating excellent photocatalytic activity and stability of the MnTD incorporated MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ catalyst toward water oxidation. In contrast, homogeneous MnTD without MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ showed very poor stability and ceased to work after $400\ \mathrm{s}$ This photocatalytic system was further developed by replacing the oxidant (K-oxone) with CAN by the same group.[108] Remarkably, a high turnover frequency (TOF) of $40~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ (mol $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ per mol MnTD) and desirable photo­catalytic stability under harsh water oxidation conditions $(\mathrm{pH}=1$ ) over more than $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ were delivered using CAN as oxidant. In this work, the superiority of confining one MnTD mole­cule within one pore of MIL-101(Cr) was also demonstrated by comparison with FDU-12, mesoporous silica with large pores that can accommodate more than one MnTD molecules within one pore, which showed much poorer stability (Figure 8C).
Other MOFs, such as a Bi-based $\mathrm{MOF^{[109]}}$ and some Fe-based MOFs,[110–112] also exhibited photocatalytic activity toward water oxidation.
## 3. Electrochemical Fuel Production and Water Oxidation
Compared with photochemical ones, the number of the works reported on MOFs for electrochemical fuel production and water oxidation is relatively limited, which may be due to the poor electrical conductivity and limited mass permeability of MOFs. MOFs, MOF composites and their derived materials for electrocatalysis of hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and water oxidation have been recently reviewed and discussed in detail by us and others.[37,40] Herein, meritorious examples of MOFs for these electrochemical applications were briefly presented aiming to provide the readers with inspirations to develop MOFs and MOF composites with new properties and chemistries for efficient and durable electrocatalysis of hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and water oxidation.
## 3.1. Electrochemical Hydrogen Production
The state-of-the-art electrocatalysts for hydrogen production from water have always been the Pt-based nanomaterials, whose widely utilization is hindered by the high cost and scarcity of Pt.[113] POMs and their composites have been investigated as promising Earth-abundant electrocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction, however, the high solubility and low surface area limited their electrochemical performance.[114–117] In this regard, Dolbecq and co-workers synthesized three POM-based MOFs (POMOFs) by using tetrahedral $\varepsilon$ -Keggin POMs as node and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate as linkers.[118] Among these three POMOFs, $(\mathrm{TBA})_{3}[\mathrm{PMo^{V}_{8}M o^{V I}_{4}O_{36}(O H)_{4}Z n_{4}}]$ $[\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{3}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]_{4/3}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3})$ exhibited the best electrochemical performance for hydrogen production with an onset potential of around $+20\ \mathrm{mV}$ versus saturated calomel electrode (SCE) in a $(1\mathrm{~M~LiCl+HCl}$ $\mathrm{(pH=1)}$ ) solution. However, the tetrabutylammonium (TBA) counterions may block the channels and pores of $\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3}$ , thus block the electrochemically active sites and hinder the mass transport. To synthesize POMOFs with permanent porosity, benzene tribenzoate was used as linkers to construct a POMOF (NENU-500) with excellent chemical stability, relatively high porosity, and abundant exposed electrochemically active sites by Zhou and co-workers (Figure 9A).[119] The resulting NENU-500 exhibited excellent electrochemical performance toward hydrogen generation with onset overpotential of $180~\mathrm{mV}$ and overpotential of $237~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ as well as a Tafel slope of $96~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which was much better than those of $\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3}$ and other POMOF counterparts (Figure 9B,C). The remarkable hydrogen generation electrocatalytic performance can be attributed to the porosity of NEUN-500 that facilitate the mass transport to the accessible catalytically active POM moieties.
In other cases, Hupp and co-workers in situ grew the rod-like $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF, NU-1000, on the electrically conductive FTO glass substrate, and then electrodeposited Ni–S at the bottoms of the NU-1000 rods.[120] It was found that the NU-1000 can modify the immediate environment of the hybrid film, leading to promoted local proton delivery and/or transport. As a result, the hybrid film with both NU-1000 and Ni–S exhibited much enhanced electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution performance than the films with only NU-1000 or Ni–S. This work offered a new concept that MOFs can be used to modify the electrochemical environment of the electrocatalysts to improve their electrocatalytic performance.
## 3.2. Electrochemical $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
The first example employing MOFs for electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction was presented by Yamada and co-workers in 2012.[121] They synthesized a copper rubeanate MOF which can electrochemically reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to HCOOH with an onset potential of $0.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ more positive than that of the benchmarking Cu metal electrode. After that, Yang and co-workers synthesized a thin film of nanosized cobalt-porphyrin based MOF, $\mathrm{\Al_{2}(O H)_{2}T C P P}$ - Co, for efficient and selective electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[122] The resulting $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-C}$ o thin film delivered a selectivity of $76\%$ toward CO generation and a high TON of 1400 as well as a desirable stability over $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ . Another porphyrin-based MOF (Fe_MOF-525) nanosized thin film also exhibited high electrocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction due to the high areal concentration of the active Fe-TPP on the electrode surface as well as the regular nanoporosity for facile mass transport to the accessible active sites.[123]
In other cases, Han and co-workers deposited sheet-like ZnMOFs $_{(Z\mathrm{n}-1,3,5}$ -benzenetricarboxylate) onto the carbon papers, which can be used as cathodes for selective and efficient electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the imidazolium based ionic liquids electrolytes.[124] Highly oriented Re-complexes incorporated MOF thin films were deposited onto conductive FTO substrates by Sun and co-workers, which can selectively and efficiently reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with high faradaic efficiency of ${\approx}93\%$ for CO production.[125]
## 3.3. Electrochemical Water Oxidation
Some Co-based MOFs such as $\mathrm{Co(L)}_{0.5}(\mathrm{adip)}$ ,[126] ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(5-$ $\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}2\mathrm{O})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{[126]}$ $\mathrm{L}=1$ ,4-bis(3-pyridylaminomethyl)­benzene, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{adip}}=$ adipic acid) and Co-ZIF-9[127] have shown electrocatalytic activities toward water oxidation when directly used as active materials. In more complicated cases, MOFs were used as hosts to support $\alpha{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles[128] and Co species,[129] or were combined with conductive substrates such as $\mathrm{Ti}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{CdS}$ nanowire array substrates,[130] Ni foams,[131] and $\mathrm{CNTS^{[132]}}$ for efficient and durable electrocatalysis of water oxidation.
Recently, Tang and co-workers prepared ultrathin NiCo bimetallic MOFs nanosheets (NiCo-UMOFNs) with abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal sites by a simple ultrasonic method (Figure 9D–G).[133] The resulting NiCo-UMOFNs exhibited remarkably enhanced electrocatalytic activity toward water oxidation with a more negative onset potential of $1.42\mathrm{~V~}$ versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), a lower operational overpotential of $250~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a smaller Tafel slope of $42\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ than those of the monometallic counterparts Ni- and Co-UMOFNs as well as the bulk NiCo-MOFs (Figure 9H–I). The impressive electrochemical performance of the NiCo-UMOFNs was ascribed to the nanosized ultrathin morphology for facile mass transport and fast electron transfer, the abundant accessible coordinatively unsaturated metal sites with high catalytic activity, and the coupling effect between Co and Ni in the ordered MOF structure. This study offers an efficient strategy to design MOFs in atomic precision for electrocatalysis of water oxidation.
## 4. Supercapacitors
Supercapacitors, as emerging promising energy storage devices, have attracted increasing attention due to their higher power densities, faster charge–discharge rates, and longer cycling lives than conventional rechargeable batteries.[8,10,134] MOFs and MOF composites have shown great potentials for supercapacitors, especially for electrochemical double layer capacitors (EDLCs, Figure 10) due to the highly porous nature, which is, however, always coupled with poor electrical conductivity and low structure stability for conventional MOFs.[135,136] Overcoming these intrinsic disadvantages and making full use of the high surface area of pristine MOFs during electrochemical process is of great importance as the widely used activated carbon can only deliver limited theoretical capacitance for EDLCs. In the following sections, we summarized typical examples and development strategies of pristine MOFs and MOF composites for supercapacitors (Figure 10), and hopefully will bring out new designs and synthetic ideas for future works on MOF-based supercapacitors.
## 4.1. Pristine MOFs for Supercapacitors
There are several works claiming the utilization of MOFs for supercapacitors applications in aqueous electrolyte.[34,137] However, the actual active materials on the working electrode are the MOF-derived materials (e.g., functionalized nickel hydroxides or oxides) instead of the MOFs themselves. Up to now, only a few classic MOFs can remain intact in conventional aqueous electrolytes (KOH, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4},$ ) because the skeletons of the conventional MOFs will collapse in both acid and alkaline environments. Thus, organic electrolytes are more favorable for pristine MOFs-based supercapacitors. Yaghi and co-workers reported synthesis of 23 nanosized conventional MOFs (nMOFs) with different pore sizes and shapes, and applied them as active materials of supercapacitors electrodes in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ tetraethylammonium tetrafluoroborate $((\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5})_{4}\mathsf{N B F}_{4})$ –acetonitrile electrolyte[138] (The diameter of $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{4}\mathrm{N}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ is 0.68 and $0.33\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.[139]) To overcome the poor conductivities of these nMOFs, the nMOFs were mixed along with graphene in hexane to make a dispersion, which was then coated on the cleaned substrates in spin coater to give a nMOF film of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness (Figure 11A,B). By comparing the areal capacitance of different series of nMOFs, the author listed three aspects that may affect the performance of the nMOF-based supercapacitors: (1) the introduction of multiple metal ions (MOF-74 series) will help enhance the performance as the redox behavior is amplified in terms of the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and galvanostatic charge/discharge curves; (2) $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ nitrogen atoms in the ligand can increase the interaction with ions in the UiO series MOFs; (3) most importantly, the particle and pore size of the as-synthesized MOFs must be appropriate. The best sample the authors found is the nMOF-867 whose topology structure is the same as nUiO-76 but with $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine instead of biphenyl as the ligand. The gravimetric capacitance can be as high as $726\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ , which is much better than those of commercial activated carbon and graphene.
The best assumption is to synthesize highly conductive MOFs with rational pore distribution as pristine MOFs have the potential to be a better active material in organic electrolyte than commercial activated carbon from Yaghi’s results. Recent work from Dinca˘ and co-workers opened up the road to intrinsically conductive MOFs for supercapacitors.[140] By using the ligand 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP) along with Ni ion, the authors successfully obtained $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ MOF with high bulk electrical conductivity of ${>}5000\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{m}^{-1}$ , higher than those of activated carbons and graphite $(\approx1000\ \mathrm{S\m^{-1}})$ . This conductive MOF is composed of stacked $\pi$ -conjugated 2D layers, which are penetrated by 1D cylindrical channels (Figure 11C,D). The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption measurement and corresponding pore size distribution further revealed the diameter of these channels to be ${\approx}1.5~\mathrm{nm}$ , which was sufficiently spacious to accommodate large organic electrolyte ions in EDLCs. The resulting conductive $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ were used as active materials to assemble a two-electrode symmetric device with $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}/A C N}$ as electrolyte. The areal capacitance normalized to specific surface area was calculated to be $18~\upmu\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is higher than those of any carbonaceous materials except graphene (Figure 11E,F). The cycling performance was also impressive as the capacitance retention can be as high as $90\%$ after 10 000 cycles, and the chemical stability of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was confirmed by X-ray absorption spectroscopy characterizations. The combination of highly porous nature, excellent electrical conductivity, desirable electrochemical stability in organic electrolyte, and large open channels that could enable swift electrolyte movement without causing volume changes during galvanostatic charge/discharge cycling, helps to explain the impressive electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ -based supercapacitors.
## 4.2. MOF Composites for Supercapacitors
Up to now, many works have been done on composite materials using 1D or 2D materials as constituent materials for supercapacitors. However, materials without 3D architectures usually suffer from a stacking problem during electrochemical cycles.[141,142] MOFs with intrinsic 3D porous architectures could potentially be utilized as one of the constituent materials of the composite materials for EDLCs. The electrochemical performance of the MOF composites could be optimized by choosing appropriate constituent materials.
Generally, MOF composites for supercapacitors can be classified based on the different roles that MOFs played, because MOFs can be regarded either as active components or substrates during electrochemical charge/discharge processes. In the prior case where MOFs are utilized as active components that contribute to the total capacitance, two key problems are encountered: lack of electrical conductivity and chemical stability, which can be overcome by combining
MOFs with conductive constituents such as conductive polymers like polyaniline (PANI) or conductive carbon materials like graphene and carbon nanotube (CNT), and judiciously selecting MOFs with high chemical stability and appropriate electrolytes.
Sawangphruk and co-workers synthesized a composite of $10\mathrm{\mt}\%$ reduced graphene oxide (rGO) and HKUST-1 to improve the conductivity of pure HKUST-1.[143] Besides, the introduction of rGO can lead to a pore size distribution containing both micropores and mesopores with an average pore diameter of $8.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is desirable for uptaking and releasing electrolyte ions. When tested in three-electrode configuration with 0.5 m $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ as electrolyte, the resulting composite exhibited a desirable specific capacitance of $385\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ , whereas the pure HKUST-1 without rGO stored very limited charges. CNT is another carbon material used to improve the conductivity of the MOF composites. Lin and co-workers synthesized a composite material of CNTs and Mn-MOF $(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{C}_{2}$ $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4})$ .[144] The incorporation of CNTs led to an enhanced capacitance from $43.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ for pure Mn-MOF to $203.1\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ for the Mn-MOF/CNT composite in 1 m $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . Researchers also synthesized ternary composite of MOF, conductive carbon, and conductive polymer. For example, Wang and co-workers interwove ZIF-67 crystals, which were grown on carbon cloth, with electrochemically deposited PANI chains (PANI-ZIF-67-CC) to improve the conductivity of the ternary MOF composite and facilitate the Faradaic processes across the interface (Figure 12A,B).[145] Much enhanced areal capacitance of $2146~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ at $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ for PANI-ZIF-67-CC in a three-electrode configuration was achieved compared with the binary ZIF-67-CC without PANI and PANI-CC without ZIF-67. Besides, $1\mathrm{~M~H_{2}S O_{4}/p o l y}$ (vinyl alcohol) (PVA) gel electrolyte which ensured the stability of pristine ZIF-67 during galvanostatic charge/discharge processes was used for a flexible allsolid-state symmetric device, yielding an areal capacitance of $35~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ at a current density of $0.05\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ along with good cycling stability (Figure 12C,D). Fu and co-workers synthesized HKUST-1-GO hybrids and further electrochemically deposited poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) (PEDOT) to get a MOF-based ternary composite.[146] Electrochemical studies showed that the resulting ternary composite exhibited an areal capacitance of $128\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ at $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ in $3\mathrm{~M~KCl}$ which surpassed the binary HKUST-1-rGO or PEDOT-HKUST-1. When fabricated and tested in a symmetric solid-state supercapacitor with $\mathrm{H_{3}P O_{4}/P V A}$ as the gel electrolyte at $5~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , the device showed an exceptionally high areal capacitance of $37.8~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ . In conclusion, to make full use of the inherent merits of MOFs such as the highly porous nature, combining them with highly conductive materials to compensate for the poor conductivity of conventional MOFs is an efficient development strategy in supercapacitors. The electrochemical performances of the MOF composites will be enhanced in terms of capacitance, rate performance, and cycling stability compared to those of pristine MOFs as the conductivity, pore size distribution, and sometimes the morphology of the MOF composite materials are optimized.
The development strategies will be altered if MOFs are regarded as substrates, which contribute negligibly to the total capacitance of the composites. Researchers are more likely to introduce pseudocapacitive or battery-type materials into the MOF frameworks in this case. Conductive polymers with redox active (e.g., PANI) and metal oxides/hydroxides are the most commonly used pseudocapacitive/battery-type materials, which can be incorporated in MOFs to afford MOF composites for supercapacitors. Sapchenko and co-workers reported the synthesis of PANI on MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ and used the resulting composite $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{PANI}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr}))}&{{}}\end{array}$ as active material for supercapacitors.[147] The electrical conductivity of the $\mathrm{PANI}@\mathrm{MIL}$ $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ composite could reach $10^{-6}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . When tested in a three-electrode configuration in 1 m $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , the shape of the CV curve and the electrochemical behavior of the $\mathsf{P A N I@M I L}$ - $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ were quite similar to those of pristine PANI. A capacitance of $135\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ was delivered at $1\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ , which was smaller than the capacitance of pure PANI as the MOF substrate contributed to the total mass of the active material. Besides conductive polymers, nickel hydroxide derivatives, a typical battery-type material, can also be incorporated into MOFs. Tang and co-workers synthesized $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ on the surface of $Z\mathrm{r-UiO-}66$ $(\mathrm{Ni_{3}}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}.66)$ .[148] This time the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\textcircled{\scshape A}$ UiO-66 composite material exhibited higher capacitance of $992\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ than the individual $\mathrm{Zr}$ -UiO-66 $(\approx134\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ $(\approx753\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ at $5~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in $6\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ . The enhanced performance of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}@$ UiO-66 composite was attributed to the high surface area $(1073~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of the as-synthesized UiO-66, which maximized the dispersion of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ and further led to the advantageous nanosheet morphology.
MOFs can also serve as both coeffective active materials and substrates in one MOF composite for supercapacitors. For example, Chen and co-workers successfully prepared a duallayer composite composed of a MOF structure (NiHCF) and $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ (denoted as ${\mathrm{NiHCF}}@{\mathrm{MnO}}_{2})$ as electrode materials of supercapacitors.[149] The specific capacitance of the ${\mathrm{NiHCF}}@$ $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ composite was larger than those of single NiHCF and $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ due to the synergistic effect between NiHCF and $\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ , which was indicated from both CV and galvanostatic charge/ discharge curves of the dual-layer composite. The capacitance of this composite from three-electrode test can reach $224\textup{F}\ g^{-1}$ at $50~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , much higher than those of individual NiHCF $(177.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ $(172\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ . Inspired by this work, and to further improve the electrochemical performance, Huang and co-workers tried to make more intimate connection between the pseudocapacitive materials and MOFs.[150] $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ was in situ induced from the parent Mn-MOF (MHCF) through a one-step self-transformation process (Figure 12E,F). The resulting $\operatorname{MnO}x$ -MHCF composite exhibited much enhanced capacitance $(\approx1200\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ compared with pristine MHCF $(\approx300\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ in 1.0 m ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ (Figure 12G,H). Besides, the rate performance as well as the cycling stability were also prominently improved at the same time.
## 5. Li-Based Batteries
Li-based batteries here refer to Li-ion batteries, Li–S batteries and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries. Since the successful commercialization of Li-ion batteries (LIBs) by Sony Inc. in 1991, LIBs have been widely used in portable electronics because of the long cycle life, large voltage, and sufficient energy and power density.[151] However, to power the future electrical vehicles (EVs), higher energy and power density are needed, which require development of advanced anode, cathode and electrolyte materials with desirable electrochemical performances.[152,153] Despite many critical challenges remaining to be overcome, Li–S batteries and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries with much higher theoretical energy densities than conventional LIBs are considered promising as next generation electrochemical energy storage devices.[11] Although the electrochemical processes are not completely the same, MOFs, due to the diversity of the organic linkers and metal nodes, the tunable pore structures as well as the controllable electrochemical properties, have been employed in all of these three Li-based batteries (Figure 13).
## 5.1. Li-Ion Batteries
## 5.1.1. Anode Materials
In commercial LIBs, graphite, whose Li ion storage is based on reversible intercalation and de-intercalation of Li ion into its inter-layers, remains the best choice for anode materials because of its low working potential, low cost, and relatively long cycle life.[11] However, the low theoretical capacity of $372\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and the sluggish Li ion transport rate of graphite result in low energy and power density, which are not able to meet the requirement of future EVs.[154] Great efforts have been made to develop advanced anode materials of LIBs with high energy and power density, long cycle life, and low cost, which can be divided into 3 categories according to their Li ion storage mechanisms: materials that store and release Li ion (1) through reversible insertion and de-insertion reactions (insertion-type materials), such as graphite and titania, (2) through reversible conversion reactions (conversion-type materials), including transition metal oxides and sulfides, (3) through Li alloying and de-alloying reactions (alloying-type materials), such as Si and Sn.[155] MOFs were also widely investigated as anode materials of LIBs. According to the aforementioned Li ion storage mechanism, some of the MOFs fall into the category of conversion-type anode materials undergoing the conversion reactions where transition metal ions in these MOFs are replaced by Li ions and reduced to the zero oxidation state. During the conversion reaction, the framework structure of MOF decomposes, followed by formation of metal during the discharge process and, if any, subsequent alloying reaction, where Li forms alloy with the resulting zero oxidation state metal (e.g., Zn and Cu). The other MOFs belong to insertiontype anode materials. Li ions are allowed to be inserted into/ extracted from the nanopores of this type of MOFs without formation of zero oxidation state metal. There are many strategies to develop MOFs and MOF composites for anode materials of LIBs with improved reversible capacity, rate performance, and cyclability, including judicious selection of organic linkers and metal centers, reducing particle size to nanoscale, developing smart morphology, thermal activation, and combining MOFs with conductive substrate (Figure 14).
Conversion Reaction (and Subsequent Alloying Reaction): MOF177 $(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BTB})_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DEF})m(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})n$ , $\mathtt{B T B}=1,3,5$ -benzenetribenzoate, DEF $\mathop{=}N,N$ -diethylformamide) was the first MOF used as anode materials for LIBs.[156] This $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF underwent conversion reaction and subsequent alloying reaction (1)–(3)
$$
\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{(BTB)}_{2}\cdot\mathrm{(DEF)}m\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}n+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{Li}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{Zn}+\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{{DEF+H_{2}O+e^{-}+L i^{+}\rightarrow L i_{2}(D E F)+L i O H}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Zn+Li^{+}+e^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{LiZn}}
$$
The irreversible reaction (1) resulted in destruction of the framework structure of MOF-177 and the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O$ ， leading to poor cyclability with a great capacity loss from $400\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ in the first cycle to $105\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ in the second cycle.
To improve the long-term cyclability, a reversible conversion reaction where transformation and regeneration of the MOF structure should be introduced, which can be achieved by judicious selection of the organic linkers. For example, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]$ , using formic acid as ligand, underwent reversible conversion reaction and subsequent alloying reaction when used as anode materials for LIBs by Vittal and co-workers (reactions (4) and (5))[157]
$$
\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\left(\mathrm{HCOO}\right)_{6}+6\mathrm{Li}^{+}+6\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{Zn}+6\mathrm{HCOOLi}
$$
$$
3\mathrm{Zn}+3\mathrm{Li}^{+}+3\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{LiZn}
$$
The formation of lithium formate rather than $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O$ (irreversible product in the case of MOF-177) was proved both theoretically and experimentally to be thermodynamically preferred. On discharge, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]$ reacted with Li to form $Z\mathrm{n}$ nanoparticles and lithium formate, and the resulting $Z\mathrm{n}$ nanoparticles alloyed with Li when deeply discharged to $0.005\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ . On charge, the reverse reactions took place. Because of the reversible reactions upon cycling, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]$ exhibited an improved cyclability: $560~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after more than 60 cycles at $60\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Apart from $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs, a Mn-based layered MOF $(\mathrm{[Mn-(tfbdc)(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}b p y)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ , Mn-LCP) constructed with $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ ions and two kinds of ligands (2,3,5,6-tetrafluoroterephthalatic acid, tfbdc and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy) was also synthesized and used as the anode material for LIBs by Liu et al.[158] It exhibited a capacity of $390~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ from the forth cycle. Similar to $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]$ , Mn-LCP stored Li through a conversion reaction where metallic Mn and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}(\mathrm{tfbdc})(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})$ was formed. The cycling stability and the rate performance of this kind of tfbdc and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy built layered MOFs were further improved by reducing the particle size to nanoscale by the same group. They prepared $\mathrm{[Zn(tfbdc)(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}b p y)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ nanosheets with thickness of $24\pm8$ nm, showing an enhanced capacity of $632~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles along with excellent cycling stability and high rate performance.[159]
Insertion Reaction: In addition to conversion reaction and the subsequent alloying reaction, MOFs can also store Li through insertion reaction (insertion-type MOFs). The permanent pores of MOFs can allow Li ions to be inserted into and extracted from, and the regular channels can facilitate Li ion diffusion. Moreover, the transition metal ions and the organic linkers can act as redox active sites, while the organic linkers can also provide abundant Li ion insertion sites. The structure of the insertion-type MOF is prone to remain intact during the insertion and deinsertion of Li ion, which can lead to good cycling stability and small volume change during cycles.
Insertion Reaction—Linker Selection: Most of the investigated insertion-type MOFs are constructed with carboxylic acids containing aromatic rings as organic linkers (e.g., terephthalic acid and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid), because not only both $\mathrm{COO-}$ group and aromatic ring can serve as Li insertion sites but also the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction will help stabilizing the 3D network of the MOF, leading to high capacity and cyclability. Tarascon and co-workers synthesized a Li-based MOF, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Li terephthalate), which exhibited a reversible capacity of about $300~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ with slow attenuation to $234~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles.[160] Using bulkier aromatic linkers with similar orientated carboxylate groups like 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylic acid (2,6-NDC) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid (4,4′-BPDC) can lead to other Li-based MOFs with same topology as $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .[161] Among these linkers, Ogihara and co-workers proposed that 2,6-NDC is preferable to build Li-based MOFs for anode materials of LIBs in terms of both electrostatic stability and specific capacity.[162] They demonstrated two models for the Li intercalation state of 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylate dilithium (2,6-Naph $(\mathrm{COOLi})_{2})$ , where model 1 exhibits that a distorted tetrahedral $\mathrm{LiO}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ structure is formed and the intercalated Li ions are stabilized in their respective layers, while model 2 shows that the intercalated Li ions are located in the stacking naphthalene layers (Figure 15A,B). Storing Li ions through these two models, the $2,6\mathrm{-Naph}(\mathrm{COOLi})_{2}$ exhibited higher energy density than $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ along with a remarkably small volume change of $0.33\%$ during Li intercalation. Considering that the formation of structure similar to these 2 models is difficult for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ because of the electrostatic repulsion, and that the bulkier linkers would lower the theoretical specific capacity, it was concluded that 2,6-NDC is an ideal option among the carboxylic acids containing aromatic rings to build Li-based MOFs for anode materials.
MOFs built with nitrogen-containing organic linkers were also investigated as anode materials because nitrogen atoms in linkers can interact with Li ion and serve as insertion sites to store Li. Chen and co-workers synthesized a bifunctionalized MOFs (BMOFs), $\mathrm{Zn(IM)}_{1.5}(\mathrm{abIM)}_{0.5}$ , with hydrophobic phenyl and polar amine functionalities (IM and abIM refer to imidazole and 2-aminobenzimidazole, respectively, Figure 15C).[163] Based on DFT calculations, 4 potential Li insertion sites were proposed, including IM rings, benzene rings, IM rings in abIM and amine groups (Figure 15D). The abundant Li insertion sites together with the stable frameworks structure which remained intact during charge–discharge cycles endowed $\mathrm{Zn(IM)}_{1.5}(\mathrm{abIM)}_{0.5}$ with good Li storage capacity, cyclability, and rate performance. In other cases, Hu and co-workers synthesized a Co-based MOF constructed with azide-containing linkers, which exhibited good cyclability with discharge and charge capacities of 510.4 and $508.4\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}$ respectively after 200 cycles at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ .[164] Zheng and co-workers synthesized a flexible, wavy layered Ni-based MOF constructed with $3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetramethyl-4, $4^{\prime}$ -bipyrazole $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}\right)$ as linkers.[165] The two pyrazolate rings of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ can rotate along the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ single bond, thus offer this MOF excellent flexibility to adjust the frameworks variation during Li insertion and deinsertion, leading to good cycling performance.
Insertion Reaction—Metal ions Selection: Apart from organic linkers, judicious selection of metal ions of MOFs can also optimize their Li storage performances as anode materials. For example, Li and co-workers used a facile cation exchange reaction to prepare $\mathrm{CaC_{8}H_{4}O_{4}}$ from the parent $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .[166] The resulting $\mathrm{CaC_{8}H_{4}O_{4}}$ exhibited lower solubility in electrolyte, higher thermal stability, and better cycling performance than $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ due to the high ionization energy of $\mathrm{Ca}$ cations which enhanced the bond interaction between the Ca cations and the organic linkers. Doping metal ions into MOFs to prepare bimetallic MOFs is also an efficient way to improve the Li storage performance. Hu and co-workers managed to improve the performance of Mn-BTC by doping cobalt to afford bimetallic MnCo-BTC with a $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ atom ratio of 1.7:0.3.[167] The cycling stability was greatly enhanced after cobalt doping: MnCo-BTC exhibited a capacity of $901\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles at $100~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ while Mn-BTC only showed a lower capacity of $251.9\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. In other cases, Fe(Zn)-BDC[168] (Fe-BDC doped with $Z\mathrm{n}$ ) and ${\mathrm{Co}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-BiCP}}^{[169]}$ (a bimetallic coordination polymer) showed better performances than their mono­metallic counterparts.
Insertion Reaction—Particle Size: Reducing the particle size has been demonstrated to be an efficient strategy to improve the performance of anode materials of LIBs because of (1) short distances for Li ion and electron transport into the anode materials, (2) large contact area of the active materials with electrolyte and conductive additive, and (3) sufficient free space to buffer the volume change during cycles.[9,170] The particle size of MOFs can be tuned from microscale to nanoscale by controlling the synthetic experimental conditions. When used as anode materials, MOFs with reduced particle size exhibit better performance than the bulkier counterparts due to the aforementioned advantages. For example, Hu and co-workers synthesized well-dispersed nanoparticles (size of ${\approx}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) of an iron-based MOF, Fe-BTC, which showed a reversible capacity of $1021.5\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a discharge capacity of $436.0\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 400 cycles at high specific current of $500\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ .[171] In contrast, the bulkier counterpart with larger particle size only exhibited a much poorer performance with capacity of $300.0\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 400 cycles at $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , suggesting the superiority of reducing the dimension of MOFs for anode materials. Similar result was also obtained in the investigation of the size effect of the UiO-66 particles toward the electrochemical performances: the specific capacity increased as the particle size of the UiO-66 decreased.[172]
Insertion Reaction—Smart Structure: Similar to reducing the particle size, constructing MOFs with smart morphology can also shorten the Li diffusion distance and improve the electrolyte accessibility, leading to enhanced performance in terms of specific capacity and rate capability. Hu and co-workers synthesized a seashell-like Co-based MOF (S-Co-MOF) using a surfactant-assisted solvothermal reaction.[173] The resulting S-Co-MOF exhibited a hierarchical layered structure like the vertical texture of the seashell (Figure 16A). When used as the anode material for LIBs, S-Co-MOF delivered a reversible capacity of $1021~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles at $100\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ and $435~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles at high rate of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , which were much higher than those of the rectangular-parallelepiped counterpart because the hierarchical seashell-like structure can not only offer cavities for electrolyte diffusion and storage but also shorten the Li diffusion distance to the internal surface. A series of CoBTC with different morphologies were synthesized using different reaction solvents by Hu and co-workers.[174] The cycling stability of these CoBTC at low current density is found similar, however, when the current density was increased to $2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , the CoBTC synthesized using ethanol as solvent (CoBTC-EtOH) with morphology of monodispersed hollow microsphere showed the highest reversible capacity after 500 cycles along with a nearly $100\%$ Coulombic efficiency. Its excellent high rate performance is due to its smart hollow microsphere morphology which can facilitate the ion/electron transport and electrolyte infusion, buffer the large volume change, and keep the structural stable during Li insertion and deinsertion. Other smart morphologies of MOFs, such as nanorod,[168] thin film,[175] and lamination-based nanosphere,[176] were also developed for advanced MOF-based anode materials of LIBs (Figure 16B–D).
Insertion Reaction—Other Strategies: In other cases, thermal activation to remove the solvent molecules (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , DMF) attached to MOFs can result in higher Li ion diffusion coefficient and improved interior electrical conductivity, thus leading to better reversible specific capacity, cyclability, and rate performance (Figure 16E,F).[177] The electrical conductivity can be enhanced by combining MOFs with conductive substrate such as graphene[178] and graphite.[166] Xu and co-workers hybridized
Co-based MOFs and Cd-based MOFs with rGO by ball milling, which delivered better electrochemical performance than individual MOFs without rGO due to the enhanced electrical conductivity.[178]
Summary: In summary, MOFs as active anode materials for LIBs can be divided into two categories according to the Li storage mechanisms: conversion-type and insertion-type. Conversion-type MOFs undergo conversion reactions with Li, forming metal nanoparticles which can further store Li via alloying reactions. Judicious selection of organic linkers is critical for this type of MOFs in order to afford reversible conversion reaction and easy transformation and regeneration of the MOF frameworks. For insertion-type MOFs, Li is stored in the insertion sites of MOFs along with the redox reactions of organic linkers or metal ions. Reducing the dimensions and developing the smart morphology of the insertion-type MOFs are efficient ways to enhance the electrochemical performance due to the short distance for Li ion to transport to the insertion site. Also, judicious selection of organic linkers and metal ions, thermal activation to remove solvent molecules, and combination with conductive substrate are also demonstrated efficient for developing advanced insertion-type MOFs as anode materials of LIBs. Despite the great progress achieved, there is still room for development of MOFs with high gravimetric and volumetric capacity, long life time, low cost, easy and scalable synthesis, and good stability under practical operation condition for next generation anode materials of LIBs, which requires better understanding of the Li storage mechanism and new development strategies for designing MOFs and MOF composites with novel electrochemical properties.
## 5.1.2. Cathode Materials
In conventional LIBs, the most common cathode material is $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ , however, its high cost, much lower practical capacity than theoretical capacity, and instability in high potential region makes it unsuitable to power the future EVs.[155] Great efforts have been devoted to the development of alternative cathode materials, including inorganic oxides containing redox active transition metal (e.g., spinel $\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ ) and organic polymers with redox active groups.[179,180] As a new class of porous crystalline inorganic–organic hybrid materials, MOFs offer great opportunities for design and synthesis of advanced cathode materials of LIBs because both the transition metal cations and organic linkers of MOFs can serve as redox active sites. Moreover, the regular channels and pores of MOFs can facilitate Li diffusion and insertion, while the structural stability upon Li insertion/deinsertion ensures the cyclability. It is desirable that both the metal cations and the organic linkers of MOFs undergo reversible redox reactions while the framework structure is maintained intact during Li insertion/deinsertion, leading to large number of inserted Li ion per formula and thus high specific capacity along with high cycling stability.
Metal Cations as Redox Active Sites: In 2007, Tarascon and co-workers successfully built an electrochemical cell using a mixture of an anhydrous iron-based MOF, $\mathrm{[Fe^{III}(O H)_{0.8}F_{0.2}(O_{2}C C_{6}H_{4}C O_{2})]}$ (MIL-53(Fe)), and $15\mathrm{\textrm{wt}\%}$ of conducting additive carbon as positive electrode and metal Li as negative electrode.[181] It delivered a reversible gravimetric and volumetric capacity of $70\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $140~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ respectively based on MIL-53(Fe). The reversible appearance/ disappearance of a Fe mixed-valence state during Li insertion/ deinsertion was demonstrated using Mössbauer spectroscopic technique, which meant that the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions in MIL-53(Fe) was partially reduced to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ as the Li was inserted upon discharge.
Although neither gravimetric nor volumetric capacity of MIL-53(Fe) is satisfactory due to the small number of inserted Li ion per formula (0.6) and its low density, this work stimulates further researches on employing MOFs as cathode materials of
LIBs, taking advantages of the redox reactivity of the transition metal cations of MOFs. Férey and co-workers synthesized an iron-based MOF, MIL-68, with same inorganic SBUs as MIL53(Fe), and tested its electrochemical performance as cathode materials of LIBs.[182] It was shown that the number of inserted Li ion per formula (0.35) is lower than that of MIL-53(Fe) (0.6) and consequently a lower gravimetric capacity was delivered. It may be due to the existence of triangular-shaped pores that are inaccessible to the electrolyte in MIL-68, which makes it more difficult for Li to diffuse into the framework structure, rather than the accessible hexagonal pores of MIL-53(Fe). Apart from iron-based MOFs, a vanadium-based MOF, MIL-47 $(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}(\mathrm{O})$ (BDC)), was also capable to store Li because the presence of the large open channels as well as the redox active tetravalent vanadium cations.[183] Similar to the redox reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions of MIL-53(Fe), $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ ions of MIL-47 were reduced to $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ as Li ions are inserted upon discharge, and the reverse redox reaction happened upon charge. Electrochemical measurements showed that $0.7~\mathrm{Li}$ ion per formula was able to be reversibly inserted into and extracted from the frameworks of MIL-47 between 4.0 and $1.8\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ at $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . MIL-47 exhibited a good specific capacity even at a fast discharge rate of $10\mathrm{C}$ , which can be attributed to the open channels for fast Li ion diffusion as well as the small particle size of MIL-47 that leaded to a large contact area with electrolyte and short Li diffusion distance.
Incorporation of Redox Active Molecules: The Li storage mechanisms of the aforementioned MOFs as cathode materials only involve the redox reactions of transition metal ions, resulting in limited number of inserted Li ion per formula and consequently a low gravimetric specific capacity. Introduction of redox active molecules into MOFs may provide more electroactive sites for Li insertion. A facile way to introduce redox active molecules into MOFs is using MOFs as the host to confine these mole­ cules within their pores and channels through host–guest chemistry. For example, Tarascon and co-workers synthesized a composite material (MIL-53(Fe)_quinone1) using MIL-53(Fe) as the host to confine the guest, redox active 1,4-benzoquinone molecules, within the channels of MIL-53(Fe) with a molar ratio of 1:1 (Figure 17A).[184] Both $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions and 1,4-benzoquinone molecules can undergo redox reactions upon Li insertion/deinsertion, and contribute to the overall specific capacity, resulting in higher capacity and larger number of inserted Li ion than those of individual MIL-53(Fe) and 1,4-benzoquinone. However, the capacity of MIL-53(Fe)_quinone $\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}_{1}$ showed a rapid attenuation. Even after only 5 cycles the capacity reached that of the individual MIL-53(Fe), resulting from the exchange between the soluble 1,4-benzoquinone and the electrolyte mole­ cules. The issue of confining redox active molecules into the pores and channels of MOFs is that the interactions between MOFs and the incorporated molecules are not strong enough to prevent the dissolution of these molecules into electrolyte during long-term cycles. From this point of view, it is more favorable to introduce redox active sites into the organic linkers of MOFs, which are strongly bonded to the metal ions to form a stable network with low solubility into the electrolytes.
Organic Linkers as Redox Active Sites: The design of redox reactive organic linkers can get some inspiration from the reported works on redox active organic polymers because the diversity of MOFs allows us to incorporate redox active functional groups of the studied organic polymers into the organic linkers of MOFs. Férey and co-workers used a tetrathiafulvalene tetracarboxylic acid $((\mathrm{TTF-TC})\mathrm{H}_{4}]$ ) with tetrathiafulvalene (TTF) cores, which have reversible oxidation redox activity involving two oxidation steps, as organic linkers to construct alkali metal-based MOFs.[185] When these MOFs were employed as cathode materials of LIBs, it was found that the TTF-based linkers showed redox activity as expected, but only the first oxidation step of the linkers was observed because further oxidation will involve the decarboxylation, resulting in only small number of Li ion inserted per linker (0.6).
Both Metal Cations and Organic Linkers as Redox Active Sites: Based on the work using $\mathrm{(TTF–TC)H_{4}}$ containing TTF cores with redox activity as ligands, the same group selected redox active transition metals (Ni and Co), instead of alkali metals without redox activity, to construct MOFs with TTF-TC as linkers in order to involve both metal cations and organic linkers as redox active sites in a single MOF.[186] However, although the three reversible redox peaks, which can be assigned to two-step oxidation of the TTF cores and the reduction of the transition metal ions $(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ to $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{I\prime}}$ ), respectively, can be observed in CV between 2 and $4\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ , these TTF-based MOFs only exhibited low specific capacity of less than ${\mathfrak{g}}_{\mathrm{\mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and only $0.25~\mathrm{e}^{-}$ insertions per formula. This is because the carboxylate–cation interaction is too strong that the ionic mobility and the delocalization of Li and electron are hindered.
The first MOF exhibiting independent redox activity on both the metal ions and organic linkers was constructed using 2,7-anthraquinonedicarboxylic acid $(2,7\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AQDC})$ as linkers and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{AC})_{4}$ paddlewheel SBUs as metal nodes with a formula of Cu(2,7-AQDC)(DMF)· $_{\boldsymbol{x}\mathrm{DMF}}$ (denoted as Cu-AQDC) by Awaga and co-workers (Figure 17B).[187] The charge–discharge curves of Cu-AQDC as cathode materials showed two plateaus within the voltage of 3.4–2.4 and 2.4–1.7 V versus Li/ ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ with specific capacity ratio of ${\approx}1{:}2$ (Figure 17C). It indicated that two distinct electrochemical processes with an electron uptake ratio of 1:2 were involved upon discharge, which can be assigned to the two electron redox reactions of the anthraquinone groups in the linkers and one electron uptake reduction from $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ to $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ according to the CV measurement. The valence change of $\mathrm{Cu}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{AC})_{4}$ paddlewheel SBUs was further proved using an in situ Cu K-edge X-ray absorption near edge structure analysis. The results showed that the discharge process involved two steps of the absorption edge energy shift, originated from the stepwise electrochemical process of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{AC})_{4}$ paddlewheel SBUs, where the SBUs got one electron in the first step to form a copper dimer of Cu (II, I) state with an average Cu valence of 1.5 and then further got another electron to be fully reduced to $\mathtt{C u}$ (I, I) state. As a consequence of the introduction of independent redox activity on both metal ions and organic linkers, Cu-AQDC delivered a high initial specific capacity of 147 mA h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ which was comparable to the practical capacity of commercial $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ $(148\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}})$ ). Despite a large capacity loss of ${\approx}40~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in 50 cycles, this first MOF with independent redox active sites on both metal ions and organic linkers still plays an instructive role in development and rational design of MOFs with multiple electrochemical active sites on both transition metal cations with variable valence and organic linkers functionalized with redox active groups for advance cathode materials of LIBs. After that, a triphenylamine-based MOF, Cu-TCA $\mathrm{\TCA=}$ tricarboxytriphenyl amine), with redox activity on both the $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ ions and the nitrogen atoms bonded with three benzene rings in the organic linkers, was synthesized and employed as cathode materials of LIBs. Remarkably, a good long-term cyclability over 200 cycles at rate of $2\mathrm{~C~}$ along with an average Coulombic efficiency of $96.5\%$ was delivered.[188]
New Electrochemical Mechanism: The capacity loss of CuAQDC was mainly caused by the highly localized electron density and the limited electrical conductivity that led to insufficient extraction of Li ions upon charge. Considering that the synthesis of MOFs with high electrical and Li ion conductivity is still a challenge to date, introduction of a new electrochemical mechanism may be favorable. For example, Awaga and co-workers discovered a novel “bipolar charging” electrochemical mechanism when using a flexible MOF, $\begin{array}{r}{{\mathrm{Mn}}_{7}(2,7.}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{AQDC})_{6}(2,6{\cdot}\mathrm{AQDC})(\mathrm{DMA})_{6}$ (denoted as Mn-MOF, $\mathrm{\DeltaDMA~=~}$ N,N-dimethylacetamide), with both the $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ cations and the anthraquinone groups in the linkers as redox active sites, as cathode materials of LIBs.[189] This “bipolar charging” process can be briefly described as follow (Figure 17D): at first the $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ ions were oxidized to $\mathrm{{Mn}(\mathrm{{III}})}$ along with the intercalation of the electrolyte $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ anions into the framework structure of the Mn-MOF upon charge. These intercalated $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ anions were extracted in the first step of the discharge process. Upon further discharge, the insertion of Li ions into the Mn-MOF took place, accompanied by the reduction of the anthraquinone groups in the linkers. This mechanism reduces the amount of inserted Li ions in one step (Mn-MOF: $1\mathrm{PF}_{6}\mathrm{^{-}}+2\mathrm{\Li^{+}}$ vs $3\ \mathrm{Li^{+}}$ in the case of Cu-AQDC), resulting in a reduced ion transport traffic and an accelerated Li desertion speed. Due to this advantageous “bipolar charging” process, and the wide 1D channels and high porosity that are capable to accommodate $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ anions without severe distortion, the Mn-MOF exhibited excellent electrochemical reactivity and cycling stability with a gravimetric capacity of $205\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and only $7\%$ of the initial capacity was lost after 50 cycles (Figure 17E).
Summary: In summary, the Li storage mechanism of MOFs employed as cathode materials involves insertion/deinsertion reaction of Li ion, accompanied by the reversible electrochemical reaction of the redox active components of MOFs. The specific capacity greatly depends on the amount of redox active sites of the MOFs. An efficient way to increase the amount of redox active sites of MOFs is introducing independent redox activity on both metal ions and organic linkers, which can be achieved by employing both transition metal cations with variable valence and organic linkers containing redox active functional groups in a single MOF. Besides, MOF constructed with metal ions and organic linkers with light molecular weight is favorable with regard to gravimetric specific capacity. High Li ion and electrical conductivity are required for high rate operation and reversible cycling stability, which are still challenges for MOFs, thus invention of novel electrochemical process and new chemistry upon battery operation is desirable.
## 5.2. Li–S Batteries
Li–S batteries have attracted numerous attention due to their high theoretical specific capacity $_{(\approx1675}$ mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for S and $\approx3860\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}~}}$ for metallic Li) and energy density $(\approx2500$ Wh $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ considering complete reaction to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})$ ), which are much higher than those of conventional LIBs (Figure 18A–C).[11,190] Besides, the utilization of the abundant and nontoxic S can lower the cost of batteries fabrication. However, the widespread commercialization of Li–S batteries is hindered by the large gaps between the theoretical and practical capacity and energy density, the extremely poor cycling stability as well as the insufficient Coulombic efficiency. These issues are mainly due to the following reasons (Figure 18A): (1) The intrinsically electrical insulating nature of S and the intermediate insoluble polysulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ ). Upon operation, the insulating solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ may wrap the S and hinder the further utilization of S. A large amount of conductive carbon additive is therefore necessary to facilitate the electron transport and the electrochemical process, resulting in low volumetric and gravimetric capacity. (2) The shuttling of the intermediate soluble polysulfides $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ $3\leq x\leq8^{\prime}$ ). Upon discharge, the intermediate polysulfides $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ $(3\leq x\leq8$ ) are formed, which are soluble into the electrolyte and can shuttle between S cathode and Li anode. The soluble polysulfides can reach and react with the metallic Li in anode, forming an insulating layer of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ on the surface of Li anode, leading to severe self-discharge behavior, and poor utilization of the active materials. (3) A large volume change $(80\%)$ occurs during the reaction of S forming $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , giving rise to pulverization of the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and thus a decreased contact area with the conductive substrates or the current collectors. Great efforts have been devoted to developing advanced cathode materials to address these issues, especially the problem of the shuttling of the soluble polysulfides.[191,192] In the early studies, porous carbon impregnated with S was used as cathode materials of Li–S batteries.[193] In these cases, porous carbon served as electrical conductive substrate to facilitate the electron transport and trapped the soluble polysulfides. However, the interactions between these carbon materials and soluble polysulfides were not strong enough to efficiently suppress the shuttling of the polysulfides, resulting in poor cycling stability over long-term operation. Recently, improved cycling stabilities were achieved by using cathode materials that have stronger interactions with polysulfides, including functional polymers, heteroatom-doped graphene and metal chalcogenides. As emerging organic–inorganic hybrid porous materials, MOFs can also be employed in Li–S batteries, either as host materials to host the sulfur and confine the polysulfides, or as separators to prevent the migration of polysulfides to anode side (Figure 18D). The electrochemical performances of recent MOFs and MOF composites for Li–S batteries are summarized in Table 3.
## 5.2.1. MOFs as Host Materials
Benefiting from high porosity, tunable pore structure and diversity of the metal ions and organic linkers, MOFs offer great opportunities to develop cathode materials with enhanced capacity and cycling stability for Li–S batteries. The high porosity can lead to large S loading while the size of the windows of these pores can be optimized to confine the soluble polysulfides. Furthermore, abundant open metal sites as well as functional groups on the organic linkers can be delivered in MOFs, both of which are capable to interact strongly with polysulfides. In the following paragraphs, typical development strategies of MOFs and MOF composites as cathode host materials for Li–S batteries, including constructing suitable pore structure, selecting suitable particle size, introducing functional groups onto organic linkers, exposing open metal sites as well as hybridizing MOFs with carbon substrates, will be discussed (Figure 18D).
Polysulfides Confinement by Open Metal Sites: The confinement effect of polysulfides within the pores of MOFs can be further enhanced by exposing open metal sites which can strongly interact with polysulfides. Based on first-principles calculations, the binding energies between polysulfides and the open metal sites of MOFs are calculated to be higher than those of other sulfur hosts, such as functional polymers, heteroatom-doped carbonaceous materials and stoichiometric metal chalcogenides.[193] The remarkably high binding energies originate from strong Lewis acid-based bondings between the nucleophilic polysulfide anions and the exposed metal cations with unoccupied $d$ -orbitals. In this regard, Xiao and co-workers synthesized and investigated the electrochemical performance of a Ni-based MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}(\mathrm{BTB})_{4}(\mathrm{BP})_{3}$ $\mathrm{BTB}\ =$ benzene-1,3,5-tribenzoate and $\mathrm{BP}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridyl, denoted as Ni-MOF), which possesses abundant exposed Lewis acidic Ni(II) sites.[198] Ni-MOF/S composite with a sulfur loading of ${\approx}60~\mathrm{wt\%}$ exhibited an initial discharge capacity of $689~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ and a remaining capacity of $611\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, revealing a high cycling stability. The high cycling stability can be ascribed to the hierarchical pore structure of Ni-MOF, and importantly, the strong Lewis acid–base interactions between the open Ni(II) metal sites and the soluble polysulfides. DFT calculations showed that only the sulfur atoms at the ends of the polysulfide chains can bond with the $\mathrm{Ni(II)}$ cations and the binding energies between the polysulfides and Ni(II) cations are in the range of ${\approx}4.5{\-}6.0~\mathrm{eV},$ which are higher than those of most of the polymeric and carbonaceous materials as well as the stoichiometric metal chalcogenides. Interestingly, open metal sites can be also incorporated into the organic linkers of MOFs using a metalloligand approach. For instance, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs with porphyrin-derived carboxylate ligand, MOF-525, were assembled by Qian and coworkers.[199] $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ and Fe(III) cations were then incorporated in the porphyrin moieties through coordination interactions with the four coplanar nitrogen atoms of the porphyrin rings to afford accessible exposed Lewis acidic metal sites in the linkers. Compared with MOF-525 without metal cation incorporation, those incorporated with $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ and ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\operatorname{III})$ Lewis acidic sites exhibited prominently enhanced cycling stability when employed as sulfur host materials for Li–S batteries. Remarkably, MOF-525 with $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ Lewis acidic sites delivered a reversible capacity of $704\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles along with a small corresponding fading rate of $0.07\%$ per cycle after $10\mathrm{{th}}$ cycle.
The excellent cycling stability of MOF-525 with open $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ sites was ascribed to the abundant Lewis acidic sites (each $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ cation can offer two accessible Lewis acidic sites) which can strongly interact with the intermediate polysulfides upon discharge/charge cycles, mitigating the shuttling of the soluble polysulfides.
Polysulfides Confinement by Organic Linkers: Apart from Lewis acidic open metal sites, organic linkers of MOFs with Lewis basic functional groups can also strongly interact with the soluble polysulfides through the interactions between the Lewis basic sites and the Li ions of polysulfides. For example, Kang and co-workers successfully introduced Lewis basic $\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ nitrogen in the organic linkers of a $\mathrm{zr}$ -based MOF $\mathrm{(Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(B P Y D C)_{6},}$ BPYDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate, denoted as nMOF-867) by using nitrogen-containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPYDC}$ as ligands (Figure 19B).[200] For comparison, MOF with the same structure as nMOF-867 but without nitrogen in the linkers, denoted as nUiO-67, was also synthesized and investigated. After sulfur infiltration, the electrochemical performances of these two MOFs were measured. The initial discharge capacities of these two MOFs were close, however, after long-term operation the retained capacity of nMOF-867 was much higher than that of nUiO-67 (Figure 19C). The prominently enhanced cycling stability of nMOF-867 than nUiO-67 can be ascribed to the two $\displaystyle\mathtt{s p}^{2}$ nitrogen atoms in each linker of nMOF-867 that can interact with the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ of polysulfides, which was evidenced by FTIR and XPS spectroscopic measurements. The interactions between the nitrogen atoms and polysulfides were also confirmed by naked-eye-visible experiments: when nMOF-867 and nUiO-67 were mixed with yellow $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ solution for $240~\mathrm{min}$ , a notable color change from yellow to transparent was observed for nMOF-867, which indicated the capture and confinement of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ by nMOF-867 (Figure 19D). In contrast, the color of the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ solution remained yellow for nUiO-67, further proving the confinement effect toward the polysulfides by the $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ nitrogen atoms in the linkers of nMOF-867.
Particle Size: The particle size of MOF also plays an important role in Li–S battery performance. Similar to LIBs, smaller particle size can facilitate the mass and electron transport and offer a larger contact area with electrolyte and conductive additive. In the case of Li–S batteries, however, the leaching of soluble polysulfides will be accelerated as the particle size decreases, which makes the situation more complex. Li and co-workers systematically investigated the influence of the particle size of MOF as sulfur host for Li–S batteries.[201] A set of ZIF-8 with different particle sizes $(\approx15,\approx70,\approx200,\approx800$ , and ${\approx}2000\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) were selectively synthesized and employed as sulfur hosts. It was found that the maximum capacity and sulfur utilization decreased monotonically as the particle size increased, but the capacities of ZIF-8 with smaller particle size dropped more rapidly (Figure 19E). As a result, after long-term cycles, the highest capacity was delivered by ZIF-8 with neither too large nor too small particle size $(\approx200\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). A model was proposed to explain the existence of such a “golden size” (Figure 19F): for ZIF-8 with size larger than a certain value $(\approx200\ \mathrm{nm})$ , a longer time is necessary for the polysulfides formed on the surface of ZIF-8 to diffuse inward and react with the sulfur inside the particle, resulting in low utilization of sulfur; for those with size too small, the external surface area is too high that the polysulfides can easily escape from the ZIF-8 host, leading to poor cycling stability. Taking into consideration both sulfur utilization and cycling stability, MOFs with suitable particle size are favorable as sulfur host for Li–S batteries.
Carbon Substrate: The electrical insulating nature of most of the MOFs greatly hinders the sulfur electrochemical process, leading to low active mass utilization and poor rate performance.[202] Loading MOFs in highly conductive carbon substrate such as graphene is an efficient way to enhance the electrical conductivity. Moreover, the carbon substrate can also serve as a physical barrier to confine the polysulfides or a freestanding flexible substrate to afford binder-free foldable Li–S batteries. For example, MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})/\mathrm{S}$ composite was wrapped using graphene sheets (denoted as GNS-MIL-101(Cr)/S) by Chen and co-workers.[203] The graphene sheets formed a conductive network to facilitate the electron transfer and served as a physical barrier to hinder the dissolution of polysulfides. EIS measurement revealed that GNS-MIL-101(Cr)/S had a much lower charge transfer resistance than $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})/\mathrm{S}$ without graphene sheets wrapping. As a consequence, GNS-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})/\mathrm{S}$ exhibited much better rate performance along with higher Coulombic efficiency. Xu and co-workers prepared $\mathbf{S}@$ M $\mathrm{IL}{-}100(\mathrm{V})/\mathrm{rGO}$ composite which showed much enhanced cycling stability than $\operatorname{S}@\operatorname{MIL-}100(\operatorname{V})$ without rGO sheets due to the physical confinement effect of rGO toward polysulfides.[204] Apart from graphene, CNTs can also serve as conductive substrate to support MOFs. Lin and co-workers prepared a flexible HKUST-1/CNT composite thin film to load sulfur into the pores of HKUST-1 (Figure 20A,B).[205] The conductive CNTs network that interpenetrated the HKUST-1 facilitates the electron transport throughout not only the HKUST-1 crystals but also the whole electrode. Besides, the porous CNTs thin film also served as a pliable and flexible substrate to accumulate the volume change upon cycles and afford a free-standing foldable electrode. A soft package Li–S battery was assembled using this free-standing $\mathsf{S}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}/\mathrm{CNT}$ thin film as cathode electrode. This soft package Li–S battery can power an LED when folded in angles from $0^{\circ}$ to $180^{\circ}$ , along with only slight capacity loss (Figure 20C,D). Moreover, an areal capacity of ${\approx}3.5$ mA h $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is higher than that of commercial Li-ion battery $(\approx3.0\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ), was delivered and can remain over 200 cycles.
## 5.2.2. MOFs as Separators
From a different point of view, since the dissolution of polysulfides seems to be inevitable, selecting MOFs with suitable pore size as ion sieves to prevent migration of the polysulfide anions to Li anode while allow the Li ion transport may be another way to address the polysulfides shutting issue. In this case, the insulating nature of MOFs in turn meets the electrical requirement of separators for Li–S batteries. HKUST-1, a Cu-based MOF with regular windows of ${\approx}0.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is smaller than the diameters of lithium polysulfides, was combined with robust GO laminates to construct a MOF $@{\cal G}{\cal O}$ thin film separator for Li–S batteries by Zhou and co-workers (Figure 20E–G).[206] The polysulfide permeation measurement showed that the MOF $@{\cal G}{\cal O}$ separator can efficiently hinder the migration of the brown polysulfides while the polysulfides can easily permeate through the GO separator without MOF, indicating the ionic sieving effect of the Cu-based MOF (Figure 20F). A coin battery was assembled with CMK-3/S composite as active cathode materials, Li metal foil as counter electrode, and the $\mathsf{M O F@G O}$ thin film as separator. It showed a very high capacity retention of $855\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ along with a fading rate of only $0.019\%$ per cycle over 1500 cycles. In contrast, the Li–S battery with GO as separator showed a dramatic capacity attenuation with a low capacity retention of only $234~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles. The prominently improved electrochemical performance can be ascribed to the ionic sieving effect of the MOF $@{\cal G}{\cal O}$ separator, which can efficiently hinder the migration of polysulfides to the Li metal anode side whereas the transport of Li ion was not affected. Using a similar synthetic method, the same group prepared a Zn-based $\mathsf{M O F@G O}$ separator which can also serve as an efficient ionic sieve, but its structural instability resulted in a poorer electrochemical performance than the Cu-based MOF $@$ GO separator.[207]
## 5.2.3. Summary
In summary, MOFs represent promising sulfur host materials for Li–S batteries due to the follow advantages: (1) high porosity of MOFs can guarantee high sulfur loadings (normally $>50\%$ ), (2) regular pores with small apertures can confine the soluble polysulfides within the pores, hindering the dissolution of polysulfides into organic electrolyte, and (3) abundant Lewis acidic open metal sites and Lewis basic site on the metal nodes or the functionalized organic linkers can strongly interact with the intermediate lithium polysulfides, preventing their dissolution into electrolyte. The variety in functionality and chemical components of the metal nodes and organic linkers together with the tunable pore structure offer great opportunities for designing MOFs and MOF composites with desirable electrochemical performances for advanced Li–S batteries in terms of gravimetric and volumetric capacities as well as cycling stability. The particle size and the conductivity of MOFs should be taken into consideration for Li–S batteries. In addition to sulfur host materials, MOFs can also serve as ionic sieves to prevent the migration of polysulfides to the Li metal anode side while exerting negligible influence on the transport of Li ion, leading to remarkably enhanced cycling stability.
## 5.3. Li– ${\bf\mathcal{O}}_{2}$ Batteries
$\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries deliver a theoretical specific energy even higher than that of Li–S batteries $(3505~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for nonaqueous $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries and $2567~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for Li–S batteries).[11] A typical nonaqueous $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ battery is constructed with a Li metal anode and a porous gas diffusion cathode using organic aprotic electrolyte with sufficient Li ion conductivity.[208] Upon discharge, Li ions that formed in the anode migrate to the cathode side with the help of the Li ion conductive electrolyte, and react with the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the atmosphere to form $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O_{2}$ (in rare cases, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O$ or $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ form as final product) at the active site of the cathode materials. During charge, the reverse reactions take place, where $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O_{2}$ is oxidized back to Li ions and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . There exist many issues in the practical operation of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries, including the decomposition of organic electrolyte, formation of Li dendrites in anode and the sluggish kinetics of ORR and OER reactions in cathode, which can cause poor cycling stability and large voltage gap between discharge and charge. For cathode materials of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries, sufficient electrical and ionic conductivity, high surface area and porosity with suitable pore size for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O_{2}$ accumulation, fast $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ transport, and abundant catalytic active sites for ORR and OER are required.[209] Porous carbonaceous materials have been widely investigated as cathode materials for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries because of the high electrical conductivity, variable pore structure, and considerable catalytic activity toward ORR and OER. However, carbonaceous materials suffer from chemical instability upon discharge/charge cycles.[208]
Because of the high surface area, tunable pore structure and robust framework structure, MOFs can also be employed as cathode materials for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries. Li and co-workers presented the first example of employment of MOFs for cathode materials of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries. The cathode was constructed by coating a mixture of MOFs, Super $\mathrm{~P~}$ carbonaceous materials and PVDF binder on a piece of carbon paper. Five MOFs were investigated, among which Mn-MOF-74 with regular 1D open channels and abundant open metal sites exhibited the highest discharge capacity of $9420~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $50\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ under $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere, much higher than that of Super $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ without MOFs $(2170\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ , Figure 21A,B).[210] The remarkably enhanced discharge capacity can be ascribed to the 1D open channels and the accessible open metal sites that lead to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ enrichment and easy $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ diffusion, which was evidenced by the extremely high $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration in the pores of Mn-MOF-74 (eighteen times higher than gaseous $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under ambient pressure). Moreover, Mn-MOF-74 showed an excellent structural stability without apparent decomposition after discharge and recharge. Chen and co-workers synthesized a Ni-based MOF with regular 3D tricontinuous microchannels, high surface area, and abundant open catalytic sites, which ensure fast mass transport, sufficient electrolyte contact, and high ORR/OER catalytic activity, respectively.[211] As a result, the coin-type $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ cell with cathode based on this Ni-based MOF delivered a high discharge capacity of $9000\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , a high round-trip efficiency of $80\%$ , and an excellent cycling stability up to 170 cycles without obvious polarization increase. A plastic $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ battery was also constructed with cathode based on this Ni-based MOF, and an energy density of ${\approx}478\ \mathrm{Wh}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ was delivered.
An ideal $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ battery is expected to be able to work in ambient air atmosphere, that is, a Li–air battery, where moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are presented.[212] The moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can enter the battery through the air cathode and reach the Li metal anode, inducing side reactions with the metallic Li and the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O_{2}$ , and leading to fast degradation and low Coulombic efficiency of the battery. One way to address this issue is using a sieve that can selectively allow the pass of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ while preventing the entrance of moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into the battery. Considering that MOFs have shown great potential as adsorbents and separators for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and gas separation respectively, it is possible to construct a MOF-based membrane with high $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ permeability while moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are blocked. For example, Lu and coworkers constructed a mixed matrix membrane (MMM) with an Al-based MOF $(\mathrm{CAU}{\cdot}1{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ coated with polydopamine (CAU-1- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA}\right)$ ) and polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA) for rebelling moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for Li–air batteries (Figure 21C).[213] The abundant amino groups in CAU-1- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can efficiently absorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ while the PMMA endowed the membrane with highly hydrophobic nature. As a result, the Li–air battery based on this MMM delivered a much higher discharge capacity and a better cycling stability than those without the MMM under ambient atmosphere with the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and moisture (humidity $=30\%$ ).
In summary, MOFs exhibit great potentials as cathode materials and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ selective separators for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries and Li–air batteries. MOFs with open channels for mass transport, open metal sites for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ enrichment and ORR/OER catalysis, and structural and chemical stability are desirable for cathode materials of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries. For separators of Li–air batteries, MOFs with high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity and hydrophobic nature may be favored. Although only limited numbers of works were reported on employing MOFs for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries, the variable pore structure and tunable chemistry of MOFs still offer opportunities for development of advanced MOF-based Li– ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O}_{2}$ batteries.
## 6. Summary and Perspective
MOFs have been widely employed in various energy storage and conversion systems because of the high surface area, regular and tunable pore structure, diversity of the organic linkers and the metal nodes, easy synthesis, and possibility to incorporate one or more functional components. We have summarized recent progress and typical development strategies of MOFs and MOF composites for energy storage and conversion applications including photochemical and electrochemical hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water oxidation, supercapacitors, Li-ion batteries, Li–S batteries, and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries. For photochemical catalysis of hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water oxidation, incorporation of photosensitive and catalytically active components such as Pt nanoparticles and coordination complexes into MOFs is the main strategy. It is possible that more than one component can be introduced into a single MOF to afford a multifunctional MOF-based heterogeneous photocatalyst for efficient and durable photocatalysis of hydrogen production, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water oxidation under visible light irradiation. For supercapacitors, intrinsically conductive MOFs with suitable pore structure may be desirable to make full use of the high surface area of MOFs. However, it is still a challenge to construct electrically conductive MOFs using common organic linkers. Therefore, combining MOFs with conductive materials may be a compromising strategy to enhance the electrochemical performance for supercapacitors, although the conductive components may decrease the gravimetric capacitance of the composites. For Li-ion batteries, MOFs can be employed as both the anode and cathode materials. In the case of anode materials, the chemical compositions of the organic linkers and metal nodes, morphologies, particle sizes as well as the solvent molecules may affect the electrochemical performance of MOFs as anode materials. As cathode materials, MOFs possessing redox active sites in both organic linkers and metal nodes are preferred, as more Li ions can be stored per formula of the MOFs. For Li–S batteries, efforts were devoted to preventing the shuttling of soluble polysulfides. MOFs with cage-like pores, open metal sites, and functional organic linkers that can efficiently confine and interact with the soluble polysulfides are favorable. The works reported on MOFs for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries are limited, which investigated the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ enrichment and ORR/OER catalysis by MOFs, and also the gas sieving effect of MOFs. Figure 22 shows the schematic illustration of typical development strategies of MOFs and their composites for energy storage and conversion applications.
For practical applications, the thermal, mechanical, and chemical stability (especially chemical stability like water, acid, and base stability) of MOFs must be taken into consideration.[214] So far, most of the MOFs suffer from decomposition and frameworks collapse when exposed to water/moisture, acid, or base, which greatly hinders the widespread utilization of MOFs for energy storage and conversion applications. However, taking advantages of the diversity in chemical compositions of metal nodes and organic linkers, many MOFs with highly stability toward high temperature, water/moisture, acid, and base have been designed and synthesized, such as ZIF-9, MIL-101, UiO-66, PCN-601, and so on.[215–219] It is expected that more MOFs with extraordinary thermal and chemical stability can be synthesized through judicious selection of organic linkers and metal SBUs, pore and surface engineering, and structure/topology design.[214,218,220–222] It is possible to find MOFs that are stable under certain practical condition, and use them as supports to incorporate active components for specific energy storage and conversion applications. For mechanical stability, special approaches (e.g., hot-press method and phase transformation processing strategy) can be used to fabricate free-standing MOFs membranes or foams with tunable flexibility and robustness, which can overcome the intrinsic fragility and limited processability of MOFs without sacrificing their pristine performances.[223–225]
As emerging crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid porous materials, MOFs offer great opportunities for designing MOFbased materials with unique characteristics for particular applications. Although there are still many challenges, it is believed that the development of MOFs and MOF composites with new chemistry and novel properties will lead to advanced MOFbased materials for more efficient, more economical, more durable, and more environmentally friendly energy storage and conversion applications.
